1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,800
Let’s look at a line of code together.

2
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:06,370
Swift once let you declare a function like this.

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:11,060
There is one function name, followed by two parameter lists.

4
00:00:11,510 --> 00:00:15,350
Pass in 2 first and you get a function that remembers it.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,000
Pass 3 to that function and you get 5.

6
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:24,300
The proposal to remove this syntax dates to November 2015.

7
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:29,710
The change was implemented in January and shipped with Swift 3 that September.

8
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:37,010
Swift once let you write two parameter lists in a function declaration.

9
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:44,100
Turning a function into a chain of functions that each take one value is called currying.

10
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:49,800
Joe Groff, the author, located the problem in the second parameter list.

11
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:54,330
If it continues the same function’s arguments, its labeling rules carry on.

12
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:58,140
Treat it as the first argument of a new function and the rules can differ.

13
00:00:58,590 --> 00:01:05,870
Groff saw little reason to keep adding rules for notation seldom useful in the standard library.

14
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:10,850
The earliest surviving proposal already says it was accepted.

15
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:16,980
In December, the migration example itself was corrected.

16
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:20,950
In the original example, the parameter appeared to be a function.

17
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:27,720
Moving the closing parenthesis made it clear the closure takes a string and returns a number.

18
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:31,020
The declaration changed.

19
00:01:31,470 --> 00:01:36,990
In place of the second parameter list, we write the returned function’s type after the arrow.

20
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:40,960
The braces below hold a closure.

21
00:01:41,410 --> 00:01:43,810
It adds the next number to the first one.

22
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:48,070
This function remembers the 2 we passed in first.

23
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:51,160
Pass it 3 and you get 5.

24
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:55,580
Even after the declaration syntax was removed, one question remained.

25
00:01:56,030 --> 00:02:03,070
Erica Sadun and Davide De Franceschi proposed putting an underscore where an argument would be supplied later.

26
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:08,560
Chris Lattner pointed out that an underscore already meant discarding a value.

27
00:02:09,010 --> 00:02:14,610
In his view, using it here to wait for a value could confuse readers.

28
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:17,700
That placeholder syntax was outside this decision.

29
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:20,680
Let’s return to the opening line.

30
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:24,730
You can still use both pairs when calling the function.

31
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:30,220
Swift 3 removed the second parameter list from the declaration.

32
00:02:30,670 --> 00:02:32,270
Let’s leave it there for today.
